A1. The best way to face the little woman. ~ BOLDLY
A6. After gaining this a burglar takes things easy for a while. ~ ACCESS
A12. Something kitchen mechanics do to potatoes. ~ PARE
A13. This turns a flapper's head. ~ AHEM
A15. Something wage earners usually get on Saturday. ~ STIPEND
A18. This kind of a person lives on Easy street. ~ OPULENT
A20. The old man, his wife, seven children and the hired girl. ~ TEN
A21. The way men get home from stag parties. ~ STAGGER
A23. Greek prefix relating to air. ~ AER
A24. These may be expected in wet weather. ~ RAINS
A26. Ku Klux baptismal liquid. ~ TAR
A27. A girl has to have patients to become one of these. ~ NURSE
A29. A medicinal plant. ~ ALOE
A30. Something small boys hurl at each other. ~ DARES
A32. Things that tennis players fight over. ~ NETS
A33. This is necessary for a real necking party. ~ NAPE
A35. An exclamation of surprise. ~ EGAD
A37. This fellow handles all kinds of cases. ~ RUMRUNNER
A39. The great divide. ~ RENO
A40. Prefix meaning half. ~ SEMI
A43. These are very sheepish. ~ EWES
A45. A necktie. ~ NOOSE
A48. The man who sold his birthright. ~ ESAU
A50. Radio bugs, golf bugs and mosquitoes. ~ PESTS
A52. A crossword puzzle fan. ~ NUT
A53. Preposition meaning downward. ~ ADOWN
A54. This is found in all picnic baskets. ~ ANT
A55. Webster says these are alarm bells. ~ TOCSINS
A58. In Hawaii this means an artificial pond in which water taro is cultivated. ~ LOI
A59. Place where bad eggs get very much worse. ~ STORAGE
A61. Scoundrels. ~ RASCALS
A63. What men leaned on in the good old daze. ~ RAIL
A64. Something butchers try to make both ends. ~ MEAT
A65. There's a lot of howling about this place. ~ KENNEL
A66. Day by day, in every way, America is getting this way and this way. ~ WETTER

D2. In most homes this is aired more than anything else. ~ OPINION
D3. This is always getting sat on. ~ LAP
D4. Abbreviated article of feminine apparel. ~ DRESS
D5. Something that all good umbrellas keep. ~ LENT
D7. Real estate with lots of water frontage. ~ CAPE
D8. Butter manufacturer. ~ CHURN
D9. The famous crossword fish again. ~ EEL
D10. What husbands get when they try to start something. ~ SMEARED
D11. This is where lost sheep go. ~ ASTRAY
D14. To emphasize or force. ~ STRESS
D16. This grows down in water. ~ TEAL
D17. Given facts upon which an inference or argument is based. ~ DATA
D18. An imaginary giant. ~ OGRE
D19. This is what love birds live in. ~ NEST
D22. Something all barbers are. (And what's more they talk too much!) ~ GARRULOUS
D25. This is the kind of bootlegger most people patronize. ~ NEAREST
D28. This is what a man is, when he hasn't a gun and is held up by three robbers on a dark street in the middle of winter. ~ UNARMED
D30. A famous kind of rum. ~ DEMON
D31. Bachelors who have this don't get married. ~ SENSE
D34. Low-brow humor. ~ PUN
D36. A juvenile exclamation. ~ GEE
D38. A meal. ~ REPAST
D39. Something hair restorers don't do. ~ RESTORE
D41. What hermits do with themselves in the winter. ~ ISOLATE
D42. Chastise. ~ PUNISH
D44. What Cleopatra did up and down the Nile. ~ WENT
D46. Where marriage is concerned, this is too much. ~ ONCE
D47. To put into motion, to disturb. ~ STIR
D49. What a lot of doughboys were when their outfit was located near Paris. (Init.). ~ AWOL
D51. An unpleasant spot. ~ STAIN
D53. This is the opposite of a liability. ~ ASSET
D56. A side glance. ~ OGLE
D57. A John Hancock. ~ NAME
D60. What most candidates also did. ~ RAN
D62. Mouse trap. ~ CAT


